    id: "cons-2025-ol-q8", topicId: "cons-2-4", conceptId: "construction-terms",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2025 OL Q8",
    questionText: "Explain, with the aid of notes and freehand sketches, any five of the following: raft foundation; housing joint; rainwater butt; stud wall; laser level; damp-proof course; door threshold; baluster; room thermostat.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "terms", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Construction Terms", marks: 50, contextNote: "Explain any five of the nine terms. Each term is marked separately: primary communication of relevant information 6 and other communication 4, in notes or sketches (the scheme reserves no fixed split between them). Raft foundation: a large, continuous reinforced concrete slab supporting the whole structure, \"floating\" like a raft; spreads the load over the whole footprint, reducing pressure on the soil; suits soils of low bearing capacity; needs more steel and concrete than a strip foundation. Housing joint: a groove or slot cut across the width of one piece receives the end of another; a medium gluing area; used for shelves, cabinets and bookcases; a through housing runs fully across and shows at both ends, a stopped housing hides the joint at the front edge. Rainwater butt: a container that collects and stores rainwater from the roof via gutters and downpipes; reuses water for plants, lawns or washing the car; reduces reliance on treated tap water and lowers metered water use. Stud wall: an internal wall framed with vertical timber or metal studs covered with plasterboard on both sides; lighter and faster to build than a block wall; load-bearing or non-load-bearing; houses electrical and plumbing services; poorer sound insulation and bearing capacity than a block wall. Laser level: a tool projecting a laser beam to give a straight, level reference line, vertical and/or horizontal; a rotary laser projects a 360° beam for large or outdoor work; more accurate than a spirit level; can be mounted on tripods or clamps. Damp-proof course: a barrier stopping moisture rising through walls and floors by capillary action; polythene, bitumen felt, lead or copper, or slate; laid horizontally between courses, typically 150 mm above ground level, and around window and door openings; protects finishes and reduces mould and decay. Door threshold: the strip of wood, metal or plastic at the bottom of a doorway where the door closes; bridges two floor surfaces and seals the doorway; external thresholds keep out weather and draughts; internal ones cover changes in floor level; must meet building regulations so they are not a trip hazard. Baluster: a short vertical post, often decorative, supporting the handrail of a stairs; a row of balusters is a balustrade; a safety element preventing falls, especially by children; also on balconies and terraces; wood, metal, glass or concrete. Room thermostat: controls room temperature by switching the heating on below the set point and off above it; keeps rooms comfortable efficiently; manual dial or digital models; smart thermostats connect to Wi-Fi and a smartphone.", group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":10,"options":["Raft foundation","Housing joint","Rainwater butt","Stud wall","Laser level","Damp-proof course","Door threshold","Baluster","Room thermostat"]} },
    ],
